          html: `<p>You have already met event streams under another name. A database's <a href="#/ch3/learn/write-ahead-log">write-ahead log</a> (Chapter 3) and its <a href="#/ch5/learn/replication-logs">replication log</a> (Chapter 5) are ordered sequences of change events, written once and consumed by followers. This chapter's big idea is to take that private log and make it public: that is what <strong>change data capture</strong> does, and it lets a search index, a cache or a warehouse follow the database the way a replica does.</p>
<p>The chapter has three parts: <strong>transmitting</strong> event streams (messaging systems, then log-based brokers), <strong>databases and streams</strong> (dual writes, change data capture, event sourcing, immutability) and <strong>processing</strong> streams (what processors do, reasoning about time, joins and fault tolerance).</p>`,
